        <tr><td>Torus</td><td>center, majorRadius, minorRadius, rotation?, majorSegments, minorSegments, axis?</td><td>3–512 and 3–256</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extrude</td><td>profile (≥ 3 points, not closed), height, origin?</td><td>profile in XY</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Revolve</td><td>profile (≥ 2 points in XZ), angle, segments?</td><td>about Z; segments 3–512, default 16</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <code>segments</code> and <code>rings</code> are minimums: <code>meshOf</code> raises them until the chord error is within <code>tolerance</code>, using step = 4·asin(√(min(1, tol/r)/2)). Two budgets stop runaway tolerances.
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Guard</th><th>Value</th><th>Error message</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>tolerance must be positive and finite</td><td>—</td><td>Mesh tolerance must be positive and finite</td></tr>
        <tr><td>segments per curve</td><td>65 536</td><td>Mesh tolerance exceeds subdivision budget</td></tr>
        <tr><td>grid rows × columns (sphere, torus, revolve)</td><td>1 000 000</td><td>Mesh tolerance exceeds vertex budget</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A Ø6 cylinder with <code>segments: 24</code> at tolerance 0.05 came out with 96 triangles (24 sides, two 24-gon caps); a Ø20 one with 128, because the larger radius needed 32 segments to stay within 0.05 mm.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="csg" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Booleans by binary space partitioning</h2>
    <p>Each operand becomes a classifier. A general mesh gets a BSP tree; a convex mesh (closed, connected, every edge convex) is just its distinct face planes, so a box is six planes and a sphere of ten thousand triangles is classified without building the quadratic chain a BSP of a convex solid degenerates to. Polygons are classified as lists, and a polygon clear of the other operand&apos;s bounding box is kept or dropped without walking anything.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>If either mesh is empty, or the bounding boxes do not meet (within tolerance), answer directly: union = merge, subtract = a, intersect = empty.</li>
      <li>Build the classifiers of A and B (convex planes or BSP).</li>
      <li>Union: keep A outside B, B outside A, and drop B&apos;s faces coincident with A&apos;s pointing the same way, so a shared face is kept once.</li>
      <li>Subtract and intersect follow the classic a.clipTo(b), b.clipTo(a), b.invert() sequence on polygon lists, without copying trees.</li>
      <li>Clean the polygon soup: weld points within max(10·eps, 1e-9·size), close T-junctions, close gaps of up to 1e-4·size between slivers, merge coplanar fragments and re-triangulate.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="csg.ts">
{"export type BooleanOp = \u0022union\u0022 | \u0022subtract\u0022 | \u0022intersect\u0022\nexport interface CsgOptions {\n  /** Distance below which a point counts as on a plane, mm. Default 1e-7 \u00d7 the larger extent. */\n  readonly tolerance?: number\n  /** Merge coplanar fragments and re-triangulate (default true). */\n  readonly mergeCoplanar?: boolean\n}\nexport const booleanMesh: (a: Mesh, b: Mesh, op: BooleanOp, options?: CsgOptions) => Mesh\n/** Union reduced as a balanced tree; subtract and intersect fold left to right. */\nexport const booleanAll: (meshes: ReadonlyArray<Mesh>, op: BooleanOp, options?: CsgOptions) => Mesh\nexport const translateMesh: (mesh: Mesh, dx: number, dy: number, dz: number) => Mesh\nexport const rotateMesh: (mesh: Mesh, axis: V3, radians: number, origin?: V3) => Mesh\nexport const mergeMeshes: (meshes: ReadonlyArray<Mesh>) => Mesh"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The triangle count grows with the features, not with the number of Booleans: the merge step puts coplanar slivers back into whole faces. In the plate above, the box had 12 triangles, after four holes 412, after the boss 540, after the bore 640; the whole chain ran in 96 ms.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ops-demo" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Union, subtract, intersect on curved input</h2>
    <p>A 20 mm cube at the origin against a sphere of radius 12 centred on its far corner (20, 20, 20), meshed at tolerance 0.2 (624 triangles). Real volumes:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Volume, mm³</th><th>Triangles</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>sphere alone</td><td>7 063.84 (exact 7 238.23)</td><td>624</td></tr>
        <tr><td>cube ∩ sphere</td><td>882.98</td><td>112</td></tr>
        <tr><td>cube ∪ sphere</td><td>14 180.86</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>cube − sphere</td><td>7 117.02</td><td>—</td></tr>
